1. HashMap ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ — "Find by hashing"
Core logic
key
 ↓
hashCode()
 ↓
hash
 ↓
bucket
 ↓
find key
 ↓
value

Conceptually:

              HashMap
                 |
        +--------+--------+
        |        |        |
     bucket0  bucket1  bucket2 ...
                 |
             key/value

Example:

HashMap<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put(10, "A");
map.put(20, "B");
map.put(30, "C");

When you do:

map.get(20);

Java doesn't normally scan:

10 → 20 → 30

Instead, it uses the key's hash to determine where to look.

Why O(1) average?

Because hashing tries to take you directly to the appropriate bucket.

Without hashing:
10
20
30
40
50
...
→ potentially scan many

With hashing:
20
 ↓
hash
 ↓
bucket
Main advantage

Very fast lookup.

Main disadvantage

It doesn't maintain a useful ordering.

HashMap<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

Think:

HashMap = "I don't care about order. Just find my key fast."

2. TreeMap ⭐⭐⭐⭐ — "Find by sorted tree"
Core logic

TreeMap uses a Red-Black Tree, which is a self-balancing binary search tree.

Conceptually:

             20
            /  \
          10    30
         / \    / \
        5  15  25  40

Keys are maintained in sorted order.

TreeMap<Integer, String> map = new TreeMap<>();

map.put(30, "C");
map.put(10, "A");
map.put(20, "B");

System.out.println(map);

Result:

{10=A, 20=B, 30=C}

Even though insertion was:

30 → 10 → 20

the Map maintains:

10 → 20 → 30
Why O(log n)?

A balanced tree has approximately:

height = log₂(n)

So finding a key involves going down only about log n levels.

The superpower

TreeMap gives you ordered operations:

map.firstKey();
map.lastKey();

map.floorKey(x);
map.ceilingKey(x);

map.lowerKey(x);
map.higherKey(x);

For example:

TreeMap<Integer, String> map = new TreeMap<>();

map.put(10, "A");
map.put(20, "B");
map.put(30, "C");

System.out.println(map.floorKey(25));

Output:

20

Meaning:

largest key ≤ 25

Main advantage

Sorted keys + powerful range/neighbor operations.

Main disadvantage

Slower than HashMap:

HashMap → O(1) average
TreeMap → O(log n)

Think:

TreeMap = "I need my keys ordered."

3. LinkedHashMap ⭐⭐⭐ — "HashMap + linked order"

This one is extremely easy once you understand HashMap.

Normal HashMap:

Hash table
   +
buckets

LinkedHashMap essentially adds a linked structure connecting entries.

Conceptually:

Hash table
   +
linked list

A → B → C → D

So:

LinkedHashMap<Integer, String> map =
    new LinkedHashMap<>();

map.put(30, "C");
map.put(10, "A");
map.put(20, "B");

Iteration follows insertion order:

30 → 10 → 20

Output:

{30=C, 10=A, 20=B}
Why still O(1) average?

It still uses hashing for lookup.

The linked structure is mainly used to maintain order.

Special feature

It can also maintain access order, which is useful for implementing LRU caches.

Think:

LinkedHashMap = HashMap + order

4. EnumMap ⭐ — "Optimized Map for enum keys"

This one is specialized.

Suppose:

enum Day {
    MONDAY,
    TUESDAY,
    WEDNESDAY
}

You can create:

EnumMap<Day, Integer> map =
    new EnumMap<>(Day.class);

map.put(Day.MONDAY, 10);
map.put(Day.TUESDAY, 20);

The important thing is:

Keys MUST be enum constants.

Internally, Java can take advantage of the fact that enum constants have a fixed set/order.

Conceptually, it can behave more like:

enum ordinal

MONDAY    → 0
TUESDAY   → 1
WEDNESDAY → 2

        ↓

array-like storage

That's why EnumMap can be very efficient.

Think:

EnumMap = Map specially optimized for enum keys.

For normal LeetCode/DSA problems, you rarely need it.

5. Hashtable ⭐ — "Old synchronized HashMap"

Hashtable is basically an old legacy hash-based Map.

Hashtable<Integer, String> map =
    new Hashtable<>();

It uses hashing like HashMap.

So conceptually:

Hashtable
    ↓
Hashing
    ↓
Buckets

But it has historical differences:

HashMap
→ modern
→ allows null

Hashtable
→ legacy
→ synchronized
→ doesn't allow null
Why don't we normally use it?

Modern Java provides better concurrency options such as:

ConcurrentHashMap

Think:

Hashtable = old synchronized HashMap.

For DSA, don't spend much time on it.

6. ConcurrentHashMap ⭐ — "HashMap designed for multiple threads"

This is about concurrency, not a different basic lookup idea.

Imagine multiple threads doing:

Thread 1 → put()
Thread 2 → get()
Thread 3 → remove()
Thread 4 → put()

on the same Map.

A normal:

HashMap

is not designed for concurrent modification by multiple threads.

ConcurrentHashMap is.

ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, String> map =
    new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

It provides thread-safe operations while allowing much more concurrency than simply synchronizing the entire map.

Important restriction

It does not allow null keys or null values.

Think:

ConcurrentHashMap = hash-based Map built for concurrent access.
